{"kind":"artifact","text":"The gs status reference states the distinction in plain English, per ISO 24495-1, at head ae8cc8a8 on main a08c32f0. A section, Two kinds of staleness, says that ordinary reasoning staleness means a basis under a record was retired so the reasoning moved while the record did not, and that it blocks nothing; and that a superseded world is narrower, means the described behaviour has been replaced, and stops a merge. It then says what the default view does with each: counts ordinary staleness per lane and marks no row, keeps finished commitments out of the lists whether or not they are stale, and keeps retired state words and superseded-world notes on their own rows with their own counts. It names the escape hatches: --all prints a stale qualifier per commitment, --json is untouched, and the bounded summary keeps its per-row stale field. This page is unchanged from head d52dfbfa, which the reviewer examined and passed on condition 3; the only edit in this round was to docs/reference/architecture.md. Its rests_on frontmatter remains byte-for-byte identical to main a08c32f0, so the clean current anchors are preserved and every edit here is prose. Tests passed: go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 uncached, all four documentation gates, and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0.","body":{"branch":"request/staleness-presentation","commit":"ae8cc8a89cdd434b7026aa0cf5af8df9a49b3217","head":"ae8cc8a89cdd434b7026aa0cf5af8df9a49b3217","path":"docs/reference/gs/status.md"}}